1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,995
Lesson 6.3.

2
00:00:02,375 --> 00:00:04,902
Determinants from work statistics.

3
00:00:06,017 --> 00:00:09,606
We already know how to extract from
equilibrium the mean, which solves

4
00:00:09,606 --> 00:00:12,682
linear systems, and the covariance,
which gives the inverse.

5
00:00:13,062 --> 00:00:15,869
Today we go one step
higher: the determinant.

6
00:00:16,249 --> 00:00:20,067
It is not in any finite moment
of the distribution, but in

7
00:00:20,067 --> 00:00:23,490
the partition function,
that is, in the free energy.

8
00:00:23,870 --> 00:00:26,105
And free energy is measured with work.

9
00:00:27,217 --> 00:00:32,314
For the potential U = ½ xᵀ A
x, the partition function is

10
00:00:32,314 --> 00:00:36,875
a Gaussian integral
proportional to (det A)^(−1/2).

11
00:00:37,255 --> 00:00:43,840
Then the free energy is F = ln
det A / (2β) plus a constant.

12
00:00:44,220 --> 00:00:49,224
If we go from a known matrix, such as
the identity, to the matrix A, the free

13
00:00:49,224 --> 00:00:53,968
energy difference is half the logarithm
of the determinant, divided by β.

14
00:00:55,083 --> 00:01:01,830
The Jarzynski equality, from lesson
3.4, says that the average of e^(−βW)

15
00:01:02,210 --> 00:01:06,676
over many repetitions of a
protocol is exactly e^(−βΔF),

16
00:01:06,676 --> 00:01:10,823
even if the protocol is
fast and out of equilibrium.

17
00:01:11,203 --> 00:01:15,493
So the algorithm is: start at
equilibrium with the identity, change the

18
00:01:15,493 --> 00:01:19,299
potential toward A while measuring
the work, repeat many times,

19
00:01:19,679 --> 00:01:26,233
and compute ln det A = −2 ln⟨e^(−βW)⟩.

20
00:01:27,350 --> 00:01:33,659
Let's simulate a matrix of dimension
five, whose log-determinant is −3.40.

21
00:01:34,039 --> 00:01:38,930
With batches of a hundred
repetitions, an instantaneous switch

22
00:01:38,930 --> 00:01:43,111
gives −2.54, with a spread
of 1.04: biased and noisy.

23
00:01:43,491 --> 00:01:49,876
With a switching time of ten units,
−3.18, with a spread of 0.32.

24
00:01:50,256 --> 00:01:55,658
With a hundred units, −3.41,
with a spread of 0.15.

25
00:01:56,038 --> 00:02:00,154
The slower the protocol, the less
dissipation and the better the estimator.

26
00:02:01,283 --> 00:02:03,456
Why isn't averaging the work enough?

27
00:02:03,836 --> 00:02:07,750
Because of the second law: the
mean work is always larger than the

28
00:02:07,750 --> 00:02:11,427
free energy difference, and the
excess is the dissipated work.

29
00:02:11,807 --> 00:02:18,709
With a hundred units, twice the mean
work gives −3.17, far from −3.40.

30
00:02:19,089 --> 00:02:22,208
The Jarzynski exponential
weights more heavily the rare

31
00:02:22,208 --> 00:02:25,496
trajectories with little work,
and that corrects the bias.

32
00:02:25,876 --> 00:02:29,583
That is why we say the determinant
uses all the moments of the work.

33
00:02:30,683 --> 00:02:32,713
This is the moment hierarchy.

34
00:02:33,093 --> 00:02:35,629
The first moment solves linear systems.

35
00:02:36,009 --> 00:02:39,001
The second gives the inverse
and Lyapunov equations.

36
00:02:39,381 --> 00:02:42,464
The full work statistics
give the determinant.

37
00:02:42,844 --> 00:02:47,668
According to Aifer and
colleagues, the complexity of the

38
00:02:47,668 --> 00:02:53,009
determinant is of order d κ
ln(κ)³ ε⁻², against d^ω digitally.

39
00:02:53,389 --> 00:02:57,124
In practice, better estimators
than Jarzynski are used, such as

40
00:02:57,124 --> 00:03:00,503
Bennett's, which combines
forward and backward protocols.

41
00:03:01,617 --> 00:03:06,890
Log-determinants appear in the marginal
likelihood of Gaussian processes, in

42
00:03:06,890 --> 00:03:12,441
normalizing flows, and in every free-energy
estimation in physics and chemistry.

43
00:03:12,821 --> 00:03:16,791
In lab nine you will compare
Jarzynski, Bennett and Cholesky.

44
00:03:17,917 --> 00:03:21,931
Common mistakes: using the mean
work instead of the exponential.

45
00:03:22,311 --> 00:03:25,059
Doing few repetitions
with a fast protocol,

46
00:03:25,059 --> 00:03:27,615
when the average depends on rare events.

47
00:03:27,995 --> 00:03:30,905
Not starting at equilibrium,
which Jarzynski requires.

48
00:03:31,285 --> 00:03:32,847
And getting the signs wrong.

49
00:03:33,950 --> 00:03:38,375
In summary: the log-determinant is
a free energy difference, and the

50
00:03:38,375 --> 00:03:43,255
Jarzynski equality obtains it from the
work measured in repeated protocols.

51
00:03:43,636 --> 00:03:47,634
Slow protocols dissipate less
and give more precise estimators.

52
00:03:48,014 --> 00:03:50,646
It is the third step of
the moment hierarchy.

53
00:03:51,750 --> 00:03:55,830
To practice: derive the free
energy of a quadratic potential.

54
00:03:56,210 --> 00:03:59,978
Explain why the mean work
bounds the log-determinant.

55
00:04:00,358 --> 00:04:02,923
Write the work of an instantaneous switch.

56
00:04:03,303 --> 00:04:06,107
And name the estimator that
combines forward and backward.
